It looks like TypeScript doesn't recognize the `process` object, which is part of the Node.js environment. To fix this error, you can install the type definitions for Node.js by running the following command in your project directory:
```css
npm i --save-dev @types/node
```
This command will install the type definitions for Node.js as a development dependency in your project. Once the installation is complete, TypeScript should recognize the `process` object and the error should go away.
Here's the updated code with the type definitions installed:
```
typescriptimport * as readline from 'readline';
const rl = readline.createInterface({
  input: process.stdin,
  output: process.stdout
});
rl.question("Enter your name: ", function(userInput) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${userInput}!`);
  rl.close();
});
```
Note that you may need to configure TypeScript to use the type definitions in your project. To do this, add the following line to your `tsconfig.json` file:
```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node"]
  }
}
```
This tells TypeScript to use the `@types/node` type definitions when compiling your project.

